  "worked_solution": "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{3}-2xy+4.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=6x,\\,b(x)=3,\\,c(x)=-x.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7-5y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,b(x)=-5,\\,c(x)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
